    ["伊斯顿·"] = "Easton ",
    ["佛尔思总是在截稿日前一夜才动笔。\n这不是拖延，而是一种奇怪的仪式——仿佛只有当死亡的阴影（或是编辑的怒吼）真正逼近时，那些沉睡在脑海中的故事才会苏醒。她窝在圣乔治区那间狭小的出租屋里，笔尖在纸上沙沙作响，咖啡凉透了也浑然不觉。这时候的她，不再是那个连出门买面包都觉得麻烦的懒散女人，而是主宰笔下人物命运的造物主。\n她写畅销小说，写《暴风山庄》，写那些冷静而智慧的侦探故事。读者称赞她的推理精妙，却不知那些让人拍案叫绝的情节，大多源于她和休在东区调查时遭遇的真实危险。生活总是比小说更荒诞，也更残酷。她把自己对危险的恐惧、对谜题的好奇，都藏进了字里行间。\n或许对远方世界的探索，一直是她命运的关键词。第一次看到穿墙而过的安丽萨太太时，那种对未知的渴望一下子被点燃了。她真的只想安于慵懒的生活吗？那神秘的力量不止是一个孤独老太太的馈赠，背后藏着无数危险——佛尔思知道的，那是来自未知世界的邀请。她将会一次又一次卷入危机，又一次次成功脱身，就像她笔下的主角一样，拥有无限危险而又精彩的人生。\n这一切或许早已注定。儿时匮乏的爱，让她习惯了以善意待人，这反而为她打开了非凡世界的大门。佛尔思早已不会怨恨谁，她知道自己远比想象中更强大，也更包容。等赚够了钱，她还要体验更多生活，写更好的故事。未来永远充满惊喜。\n毕竟，人生就是一篇等待完成的手稿啊。"] = "Fors always starts writing the night before the deadline.\n This isn't procrastination, but a strange ritual—as if only when the shadow of death (or the roar of an editor) truly approaches do the stories sleeping in her mind awaken. She huddles in that cramped rented room in the Saint George District, her pen scratching on paper, not even noticing when her coffee has gone cold. At this moment, she is no longer the lazy woman who finds even going out to buy bread a hassle, but the creator who controls the fate of the characters under her pen.\n She writes bestselling novels, writes \"Storm Mountain Villa,\" and writes those calm and wise detective stories. Readers praise her intricate reasoning, not knowing that those plots that make people applaud are mostly derived from the real dangers she and Xio encountered while investigating in the East District. Life is always more absurd and cruel than fiction. She hides her fear of danger and her curiosity about puzzles between the lines.\n Perhaps the exploration of distant worlds has always been the keyword of her fate. The moment she saw Mrs. Anlisa walk through a wall, that longing for the unknown was ignited. Does she really just want a lazy life? That mysterious power is not just a gift from a lonely old lady; it hides countless dangers behind it—Fors knows this; it is an invitation from an unknown world. She will be drawn into crises again and again, and escape successfully again and again, just like the protagonists in her books, possessing an infinitely dangerous yet wonderful life.\n Perhaps all of this was destined. The lack of love in her childhood made her accustomed to treating others with kindness, which in turn opened the door to the Extraordinary World for her. Fors no longer resents anyone; she knows she is far stronger and more inclusive than she imagined. Once she earns enough money, she will experience more of life and write better stories. The future is always full of surprises.\n After all, life is just a manuscript waiting to be completed.",
